   {
    "index": 0,
    "message": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": "**Uncomplicated high blood pressure has three equally accepted first-line drug classes, not one single drug: thiazide diuretics (water pills), long-acting calcium channel blockers, and renin-angiotensin system inhibitors (ACE inhibitors or ARBs) [1][2].** Beta-blockers are no longer first-line, because trials found them less effective at preventing stroke and other cardiovascular events [3]. The choice among the three depends on your other conditions. For example, an ACE inhibitor or ARB is preferred if you have protein in the urine (albuminuria) [4], and older adults on thiazides need electrolyte checks after starting [1]. Most people end up needing more than one medicine to reach target [2]. Your clinician or pharmacist should choose the specific drug and dose, and guidelines vary somewhat by region."
    },
    "finish_reason": "stop"
   }
  ],
